Subject: Hand back the spare pages of the copy that takes a CPU's writes up to the host

The copy that packs what a CPU wrote into a gralloc buffer for the host (0008) is reused for
every picture and keeps the size of the largest: once a video or a large picture has gone
through, its pages stay resident in the app's process until it ends, though nothing reads them
again before the next picture writes them. Once the host has the planes, the whole pages past
its first 256 KiB are marked free (dsa_spare_pages.h): the kernel takes them when the guest
reclaims page cache, and a picture written before then reuses them without a fault.

Applies after 0008 in patches/guest/mesa, on Mesa 224e91e39836d3ca31f80df2e0379e55c70574f8.
---
diff --git a/src/gfxstream/guest/android/GrallocMinigbm.cpp b/src/gfxstream/guest/android/GrallocMinigbm.cpp
--- a/src/gfxstream/guest/android/GrallocMinigbm.cpp
+++ b/src/gfxstream/guest/android/GrallocMinigbm.cpp
@@ -28,4 +28,5 @@
 #include <vector>

 #include "VirtGpu.h"
+#include "dsa_spare_pages.h"
 #include "util/log.h"
@@ -460,6 +461,11 @@
     const int status = rcEnc->rcUpdateColorBuffer(rcEnc, hostHandle, 0, 0, layout.callWidth,
                                                   layout.callHeight, layout.glFormat,
                                                   layout.glType, mUploadStaging.data());
     const int64_t sent = static_cast<int64_t>(mUploadStaging.size());
+    // The host answered, so it has the planes and the copy is dead until the next picture:
+    // its pages past the first part go back when the guest reclaims page cache, not when
+    // the app ends (a 2880x2880 picture is 32 MiB).
+    dsa::releaseSparePages(mUploadStaging.data(), mUploadStaging.size());
     if (status != 0) {
         if (!mUploadFailureLogged) {
